\id TIT
\rem MASTER:
\c 1
\p
\v 1 Paul, a servant of God and an apostle of Jesus Christ, for the faith of God’s chosen ones and the knowledge of the truth that agrees with godliness,
\v 2 in hope of eternal life which God, who cannot lie, promised before the ages of time,
\v 3 and at the proper time, revealed his word in the proclamation with which I was entrusted, according to the command of God our Savior:
\p
\v 4 To Titus, a true child according to our common faith. Grace and peace from God the Father and Christ Jesus our Savior.
\p
\v 5 For this purpose I left you in Crete, that you might set right what was left unfinished and appoint elders in every town, as I directed you––
\v 6 namely, if someone is blameless, the husband of one wife, with faithful children not accused of being degenerate or rebellious.
\v 7 For it is necessary for the overseer, as God’s steward, to be blameless: not arrogant, not easily angered, not a drunkard, not violent, not greedy for shameful profit,
\v 8 but hospitable, a lover of good, self-controlled, upright, devout, disciplined,
\v 9 holding firmly to the trustworthy message as taught, so that he may also be able to exhort others with sound instruction and rebuke those who oppose it.
\p
\v 10 For there are many rebellious people, empty talkers and deceivers, especially those of the circumcision,
\v 11 whom it is necessary to silence. They are overthrowing entire families by teaching for shameful profit what they should not teach.
\v 12 One of them, a prophet of their own, said, “Cretans are always liars, evil beasts, lazy gluttons.”
\v 13 This testimony is true. For this reason, rebuke them severely, so that they may be sound in the faith,
\v 14 not paying attention to Jewish myths or to the commands of people who turn away from the truth.
\v 15 All things are pure to those who are pure. Now to those who are corrupt and unbelieving, nothing is pure, but both their minds and their consciences have been corrupted.
\v 16 They profess to know God, but they deny him by their works. They are detestable and disobedient, worthless for any good work.
\c 2
\p
\v 1 But as for you, speak what is fitting for sound instruction:
\v 2 older men are to be temperate, dignified, self-controlled, sound in faith, in love, in perseverance.
\v 3 Older women likewise are to be reverent in behavior, not slanderers nor slaves to much wine, teachers of what is good,
\v 4 so that they may train the younger women to love their husbands and children,
\v 5 be self-controlled, pure, keeping the home, kind, submissive to their own husbands, so that the word of God may not be insulted.
\v 6 Exhort the younger men likewise to be self-controlled.
\v 7 In all ways show yourself an example of good works. In your teaching, show integrity, dignity,
\v 8 and sound speech that is above criticism, so that an opponent may be put to shame, having nothing bad to say about us.
\v 9 Slaves are to be submissive to their own masters in everything, to please them, not arguing,
\v 10 not stealing, but showing all good faith, so that they may adorn the teaching of God our Savior in everything.
\v 11 For the grace of God has appeared, bringing salvation to all people,
\v 12 instructing us that, denying godlessness and worldly desires, we should live self-controlled, upright, and godly lives in the present age,
\v 13 waiting for the blessed hope and appearing of the glory of our great God and Savior Jesus Christ.
\v 14 He gave himself for us in order to redeem us from all lawlessness and to purify for himself a people for his own possession, zealous for good works.
\v 15 Speak these things, and exhort and rebuke with all authority. Let no one disregard you.
\c 3
\p
\v 1 Remind them to submit to rulers, to authorities, to be obedient, to be ready for every good work,
\v 2 to insult no one, to avoid disputing, to be considerate, showing all gentleness toward all people.
\v 3 For we also were once foolish, disobedient, led astray, slaves to various desires and pleasures, living in malice and envy, hateful, hating one another.
\v 4 But when the kindness of God our Savior and his love for mankind appeared,
\v 5 he saved us, not by works that we ourselves did in righteousness but according to his mercy, through the washing of new birth and renewal of the Holy Spirit,
\v 6 whom God richly poured on us through our Savior Jesus Christ
\v 7 so that, having been justified by his grace, we might become heirs according to the hope of eternal life.
\v 8 This message is trustworthy. And concerning these things, I want you to insist confidently so that those who have believed in God may be careful to devote themselves to good works. These things are good and useful for people.
\v 9 But avoid foolish debates and genealogies and strife and disputes about the law. Those things are unprofitable and worthless.
\v 10 Reject a divisive man after a first and second warning,
\v 11 knowing that such a man is warped and sinning, being self-condemned.
\p
\v 12 When I send Artemas or Tychicus to you, be diligent to come to me at Nicopolis, for I have decided to spend the winter there.
\v 13 Diligently send Zenas the lawyer and Apollos on their way, so that they lack nothing.
\v 14 Now, let our people also learn to devote themselves to good works that meet urgent needs, so that they may not be unfruitful.
\p
\v 15 All those who are with me greet you. Greet those who love us in faith. Grace be with all of you.
